  "evidence": "TEXT_E: “NHS: free to all, personal to each; triple spending by 2008; cut referral-to-treatment waits to 18 weeks”; “End child poverty in a generation with tax credits, a Child Trust Fund, universal early years, 3,500 Sure Start Children’s Centres”; “A Single Equality Act and a new Commission on Equality and Human Rights; strengthen equality at work”; “Neighbourhood policing teams in every community; tougher penalties on guns and knives; tackle terrorism with robust laws while upholding liberties”; “Managed migration… a points-based system… secure borders with biometrics and ID cards”; “Smoke-free workplaces, healthier school meals, and cleaner hospitals”; “Minimum wage and tax credits that make work pay.” TEXT_F: “School discipline… empower heads to expel; Turnaround Schools”; “5,000 extra police each year; zero‑tolerance neighbourhood policing; 20,000 extra prison places; end early release”; “Secure borders and controlled immigration… Australian‑style points system… annual, Parliament‑approved limit”; “Value for money and lower taxes… reward responsibility; cut red tape”; “Bring back Matron… invest in infection control; ban junk food in schools; return cannabis to Class B”; “Recognise self‑funders… money follows the patient/pupil.”",
  "reasoning": "Both texts address care and authority, but TEXT_E foregrounds high-care and equality priorities—tripling NHS spending with 18‑week waits, ending child poverty with tax credits and Sure Start, and enacting a Single Equality Act with workplace equality—while still backing authority through neighbourhood policing, tougher penalties, and secure, biometric borders. TEXT_F emphasizes authority/loyalty and proportionality more than equality—zero‑tolerance policing, 20,000 extra prison places, annual immigration limits, lower taxes to “reward responsibility”—so it is less aligned with a profile that weights Care (0.30) and Equality (0.20) above Authority (0.19) and Loyalty (0.16).",
